<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

	<!--
		Oviparous (whole-body, attached by the reptilian gene's VEF GeneExtension).
		VEF's egg-layer comp replaces a human pregnancy with egg-laying, the Alpha
		Genes AG_HumanEggLaying pattern: progress builds over eggLayIntervalDays,
		a pregnancy fertilizes it (VEF captures the genetics and removes the birth),
		and exactly one PMM_ReptileEggFertilized is laid. With no eggUnfertilizedDef
		set, only fertilized eggs are ever laid. femaleDominant nudges the hatch
		toward daughters - momo babies are always girls either way (our assembly's
		hatch compat shim enforces the full core inheritance rules).
	-->
	<HediffDef>
		<defName>PMM_Hediff_EggLaying</defName>
		<label>oviparous</label>
		<description>Females with this gene lay eggs instead of giving live birth.</description>
		<hediffClass>HediffWithComps</hediffClass>
		<scenarioCanAdd>false</scenarioCanAdd>
		<maxSeverity>1.0</maxSeverity>
		<isBad>false</isBad>
		<!-- Visible in the Health tab as "oviparous (egg-bearer)". The bracketed half is the
		     stage label below: Hediff.LabelInBrackets returns the current stage's label, and a
		     single stage at the default minSeverity shows at any severity, so no severity is
		     needed to make it appear. VEF's HediffComp_HumanEggLayer supplies the live detail in
		     the tooltip ("Egg progress: x%", "Fertilized", or "Progress stopped until
		     fertilized"), and it keeps that progress in its own field rather than in this
		     hediff's severity. Ours is a cap only, so the number can never wander.
		     (Checked 2026-09-23 against the 1.6 VEF build and the vanilla assembly.) -->
		<stages>
			<li>
				<label>egg-bearer</label>
			</li>
		</stages>
		<comps>
			<li Class="VEF.Genes.HediffCompProperties_HumanEggLayer">
				<eggFertilizedDef>PMM_ReptileEggFertilized</eggFertilizedDef>
				<eggLayIntervalDays>9</eggLayIntervalDays>
				<eggLayFemaleOnly>true</eggLayFemaleOnly>
				<eggProgressUnfertilizedMax>0.5</eggProgressUnfertilizedMax>
				<femaleDominant>true</femaleDominant>
			</li>
		</comps>
	</HediffDef>

	<!--
		Yearly shedding. Invisible marker hediff (the hediffClass overrides Visible to
		false - the slime-jelly-oozing pattern) added and removed by the reptilian gene;
		the comp runs the countdown and drops the scales. 60 days = one RimWorld year.
	-->
	<HediffDef>
		<defName>PMM_Hediff_Shedding</defName>
		<label>shedding</label>
		<description>This reptile's scales are slowly loosening for her yearly shed.</description>
		<hediffClass>PMM_Reptiles.Hediff_Shedding</hediffClass>
		<isBad>false</isBad>
		<everCurableByItem>false</everCurableByItem>
		<initialSeverity>1</initialSeverity>
		<maxSeverity>1</maxSeverity>
		<comps>
			<li Class="PMM_Reptiles.HediffCompProperties_Shedding">
				<intervalDays>60</intervalDays>
				<scaleCount>10~20</scaleCount>
			</li>
		</comps>
	</HediffDef>

	<!--
		Constricted in a lamia's coils. Applied by the tail-grapple ability. The Moving
		offset roots the victim (5% move, held in place); the custom hediff class ticks
		tease damage from the core mod's tease system and releases the victim when its
		timer runs out, or the constrictor is downed, killed, or gone from the map, or he
		wrenches free - escape is rolled every eighth of an in-game hour (2026-09-24).
		Adjacency used to be a condition and is not one any more: the coil lasts an
		hour, so requiring the two to stay adjacent meant a lamia who took one step
		lost her victim instantly (fixed 2026-09-24). Being carried is not a condition
		either: a victim she picks up to kidnap is despawned while carried, and the
		old same-map test deleted the coil the moment the carrying began.

		Note, 2026-09-24: a probe in game read a coiled victim as downed for the whole
		coil, with full consciousness. Vanilla's ShouldBeDowned does not explain that
		by low Moving on its own - Moving only downs races whose RaceProps.doesntMove
		is set - so the cause is still open. Pain shock from the tease damage is the
		likely one. Worth probing before touching the offset here.
	-->
	<HediffDef>
		<defName>PMM_Hediff_Constricted</defName>
		<label>constricted</label>
		<description>Wrapped in a lamia's crushing coils - unable to pull free, willpower melting under her teasing embrace.</description>
		<hediffClass>PMM_Reptiles.Hediff_Constricted</hediffClass>
		<defaultLabelColor>(1.0, 0.6, 0.8)</defaultLabelColor>
		<isBad>true</isBad>
		<initialSeverity>1</initialSeverity>
		<maxSeverity>1</maxSeverity>
		<everCurableByItem>false</everCurableByItem>
		<stages>
			<li>
				<label>constricted</label>
				<capMods>
					<li>
						<capacity>Moving</capacity>
						<offset>-0.95</offset>
					</li>
				</capMods>
			</li>
		</stages>
	</HediffDef>

	<!--
		Hidden "blood running hot" flare from a flask of Dragon's Lifeblood
		(Defs/ThingDefs/Item_DragonsLifeblood.xml). Invisible on the Health tab - the
		hediffClass overrides Visible to false, the shedding pattern - and it has no
		stages, art or tooltip for a player to read.

		The effect is entirely vanilla, so no patch is needed:
		HediffCompProperties_GiveLovinMTBFactor is the comp that
		JobDriver_Lovin.GenerateRandomMinTicksToNextLovin multiplies into the gap
		between lovin', so a factor below 1 brings it round sooner (0.5 = twice as
		often). HediffCompProperties_SeverityPerDay walks the severity down, and
		Hediff.ShouldRemove (severity <= 0) retires the hediff; half a point a day
		means a two-day glow per flask, topped back up by the next drink.
	-->
	<HediffDef>
		<defName>PMM_Hediff_DragonsLifeblood</defName>
		<label>dragon's lifeblood</label>
		<description>Her blood is running hot from Dragonia's wine.</description>
		<hediffClass>PMM_Reptiles.Hediff_DragonsLifeblood</hediffClass>
		<isBad>false</isBad>
		<everCurableByItem>false</everCurableByItem>
		<initialSeverity>1</initialSeverity>
		<maxSeverity>1</maxSeverity>
		<comps>
			<li Class="HediffCompProperties_GiveLovinMTBFactor">
				<lovinMTBFactor>0.5</lovinMTBFactor>
			</li>
			<li Class="HediffCompProperties_SeverityPerDay">
				<severityPerDay>-0.5</severityPerDay>
			</li>
		</comps>
	</HediffDef>

</Defs>
